WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.430
Hello and welcome back to part two
of our video on dispersion forces.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.710
So this one we're going to specifically be
looking at dispersion, which is considered

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.570
to be the basic force of all compounds.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:15.150
We're going to try to understand
what the concept of polarizability

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:20.119
is and how this has an effect on
different intermolecular properties.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:22.100
So where does this all start with?

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:25.440
Well, when we look at all these,
this is the dominant force

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.660
for things that are nonpolar.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.600
So things like noble
gases, nonpolar molecules.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.910
But it can be found in all matter, meaning
everything has dispersion, but it's the

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.820
strongest or something like nonpolar.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.850
So what is exactly occurring here?

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.810
Well, it has to do with the electron
cloud around all these molecules.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.409
So let's say I look at a helium
atom, which is your simplest noble

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.190
gas, which has two electrons.

00:00:50.819 --> 00:00:54.840
Well, when we are trying to look
at this, well, essentially, if the

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:59.010
electrons try to disperse themselves
from one side to the other.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.560
Where we see that most of the
electrons are all piled up on the

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.650
right hand side, this is what makes an
instantaneous dipole, or a temporary one.

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:12.880
Usually this occurs when things collide
with one another, they hit each other,

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.969
um, where we have this movement happen.

00:01:16.469 --> 00:01:20.190
So whenever we see this, this
is where we have this dipole.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.760
Now, when we're looking at these,
let's say if we go from one to the

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.349
other, and let's say one of them
has a dipole, and it collides.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:34.730
With another atom or another molecule,
which is also nonpolar, it can induce

00:01:34.759 --> 00:01:39.670
even more dipoles to occur, where
they can have this weak attraction.

00:01:39.679 --> 00:01:43.280
So if you kind of notice, I had to put
in the delta pluses, delta minuses, to

00:01:43.789 --> 00:01:47.969
help like really illustrate how they
are actually attracted to one another.

00:01:48.595 --> 00:01:49.835
But this is temporary.

00:01:49.835 --> 00:01:52.464
It's something that
doesn't last a long time.

00:01:52.604 --> 00:01:56.005
The electrons will try to push
each other away because they don't

00:01:56.015 --> 00:02:01.175
want to be all bunched up on one
side of the cloud versus another.

00:02:01.224 --> 00:02:06.105
They will go back, but it's long enough
for there to be maybe a liquid could form.

00:02:06.115 --> 00:02:10.305
Maybe a solid could form for a brief
moment, or if it's cold enough and

00:02:10.305 --> 00:02:14.584
they're moving slowly enough for this to
happen where there's not a lot of energy.

00:02:14.935 --> 00:02:16.304
For this to actually occur,

00:02:25.705 --> 00:02:29.265
so to really show this, where does
this apply to when it comes to it

00:02:29.455 --> 00:02:33.765
comes to things is in the boiling
point and melting point of compounds.

00:02:34.315 --> 00:02:37.015
So here in what I'm going to show
here is going from temperature.

00:02:37.015 --> 00:02:38.525
So we go from cold to hot.

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:41.145
So red being hot, cold being blue.

00:02:41.785 --> 00:02:45.325
If we were to put things
like methane, which is CH4.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.150
It's a very simple organic compound,
and if I were to increase the size of

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.070
the molecule to, let's say, benzene,
or sorry, not benzene, but hexane,

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:02.280
which is C6H14, we have a six carbon
difference with a lot more atoms.

00:03:02.329 --> 00:03:06.150
We can see the temperatures for
the boiling point goes up by almost

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.890
200 degrees, and we're only adding
a few more atoms, but there's a

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:15.430
massive difference for nonpolar
molecules the more and more you add.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.580
For every atom, it's going
to make a difference.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.470
So one thing you can ask yourself
is, well, which one has the

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:23.109
stronger energy, like your force?

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:27.560
So the idea here is the stronger
the intermolecular force, the

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.530
more it's going to want to hold
it into the liquid phase, right?

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.110
So boiling point is all
about it becoming a gas.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.730
So it's like going back
to my video in part one.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.730
One of the things about that was,
well, if you really were to look,

00:03:42.279 --> 00:03:46.480
um, essentially through that video
was the one with the stronger

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.370
intermolecular force, which was water.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:52.600
It took a really long time
for that to really break and

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:53.850
to really see that happening.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.760
So in this case, the higher one,
the higher boiling point is the

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.249
stronger intermolecular force.

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:02.380
So it's more difficult to separate these.

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:05.230
So the things that we're looking
for is stronger intermolecular

00:04:05.239 --> 00:04:06.129
force, which is one.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:11.000
The second thing is the bigger the
molecule, the harder it is as well.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:17.620
Those two things that we're looking for at
all these different molecules other ways.

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:18.670
First, look at the size.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:20.860
Well look at, let's
look at the noble gases.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:25.740
So if we go from the top, which is helium,
down to neon to argon, tripton to xenon,

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.730
one thing that you'll see is that the
molar mass is increasing dramatically.

00:04:30.295 --> 00:04:34.565
Meaning, more electrons, more
protons, more neutrons, and also

00:04:34.565 --> 00:04:37.885
with this, the temperature of
their boiling point changes too.

00:04:38.445 --> 00:04:42.745
Helium is the lowest one, it has 4
Kelvin as its boiling point, it doesn't

00:04:42.765 --> 00:04:44.085
take long for it to become a gas.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:48.010
Whereas, if I were to fill in
the other ones, right, with neon,

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.980
argon, krypton, and xenon, you can
see that it goes up, up, up, up,

00:04:53.449 --> 00:04:55.200
up, because it's becoming heavier.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:58.100
Those electrons are harder,
essentially, it's a more of an

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.980
attraction, because there's more
of them on one side versus another.

00:05:02.559 --> 00:05:06.850
The bigger, the more attraction,
when we are trying to look at them.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:09.220
The more molar mass, the more attraction.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:14.449
So, now this is what we refer to as
polarizability, is the key word here.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:20.710
So more mass is a larger
polarizability, which means that it's

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:24.600
going to have a stronger dispersion
force between the molecules.

00:05:25.059 --> 00:05:29.599
This is important for general chemistry,
introductory organic chemistry, to

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:34.119
understand how we can predict how
chemicals will behave when they're

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:38.760
a solid, a liquid, or a gas, um,
with all these different substances.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.060
Okay, so, and you can really see how we
have heaviest to lightest, looking at

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.510
all these little individual components.

00:05:46.059 --> 00:05:49.770
Now, when we're trying to, how
can I exactly explain what this

00:05:49.799 --> 00:05:51.740
means, how does this come about?

00:05:51.839 --> 00:05:56.069
Well, this whole idea of instantaneous
dipole, well, like I mentioned before,

00:05:56.069 --> 00:05:58.150
it has to do with the electrons, right?

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:03.320
So I listed their electron configuration
here, and their total electron count.

00:06:03.814 --> 00:06:06.434
And you can see with helium, right,
that was the simplest one, two

00:06:06.434 --> 00:06:08.234
electrons, it had like four kelvin.

00:06:08.664 --> 00:06:13.275
Where xenon has 54 electrons and
went to 165 kelvin, you know,

00:06:13.275 --> 00:06:17.794
160K difference, that's a lot,
that's a big temperature shift.

00:06:18.554 --> 00:06:20.884
So how, why, how can we explain this?

00:06:21.584 --> 00:06:25.275
Well, you have to try to think of
what is the probability of all of them

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:27.525
being shifted to one side or the other?

00:06:27.944 --> 00:06:31.174
How long will they stay
attracted to each other for?

00:06:31.844 --> 00:06:36.295
So one way to think of in a really, really
simplistic model is let's say I take an

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:40.424
electron and I treat it like a coin flip,
meaning it can either be on the left

00:06:40.424 --> 00:06:42.575
side or the right side and that's it.

00:06:43.185 --> 00:06:46.065
So if I were to try to do the
probability of that, well, there's a

00:06:46.065 --> 00:06:49.954
50 percent chance it can 50 percent
chance it's going to go on the right.

00:06:50.495 --> 00:06:54.515
So if you need both of the electrons for
helium to go on one side, well, you have,

00:06:54.545 --> 00:06:59.115
you know, about a 50, 50 chance a little
bit for them to go on the same side and

00:06:59.125 --> 00:07:02.995
you can try to do this for all these
different electrons to see, okay, well.

00:07:03.575 --> 00:07:04.935
Where are they going to be?

00:07:05.395 --> 00:07:11.505
So if I were to say to make them an even
distribution, so where I have one electron

00:07:11.505 --> 00:07:15.965
on the left for helium, one electron on
the right, it's like about a 50 50 chance.

00:07:16.584 --> 00:07:22.945
Now to evenly distribute all the other
Electrons for, let's say, um, for neon

00:07:22.945 --> 00:07:28.944
from argon to krypton to xenon, it
becomes increasingly more difficult to

00:07:28.945 --> 00:07:30.995
get them to be even between the two sides.

00:07:31.724 --> 00:07:35.695
So in the, what does that mean is
that there's always going to be a

00:07:35.704 --> 00:07:40.255
little bit of an instantaneous dipole
for bigger molecules because it's

00:07:40.255 --> 00:07:43.614
impossible almost to have it perfect.

00:07:43.784 --> 00:07:46.445
So you can see xenon, it's like a
10 percent chance for it to be even,

00:07:46.724 --> 00:07:48.655
meaning most of the time it's having.

00:07:48.999 --> 00:07:51.349
attraction between other xenon.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:56.179
So, and that's one of the reasons why
we are actually seeing that difference.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.139
So, what is the pattern here in the end?

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.000
Well, one thing is, the more
electrons, or the heavier it gets,

00:08:03.459 --> 00:08:05.880
the probability of unevenness goes up.

00:08:06.365 --> 00:08:10.914
So we will always have this
instantaneous dipole for really, really

00:08:10.914 --> 00:08:14.725
big molecules because it's likely
that they're not going to be even.

00:08:15.284 --> 00:08:17.305
So this is what we mean by polarizability.

00:08:17.494 --> 00:08:22.624
It allows us to be able to predict
exactly what forces we are going to see.

00:08:22.625 --> 00:08:23.635
So the bigger it is.

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:27.800
The higher the melting point, the higher
the boiling point, which is really,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:34.210
really important when we're trying to
understand how the chemistry works, right?

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:35.659
So that is one thing.

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:39.499
So bigger, stronger dispersion
is one of the things that we are

00:08:39.499 --> 00:08:40.690
trying to get out of this video.

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:43.549
All right, let's take a
look at some other ones.

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:47.470
So going back to the example from before,
right, where I mentioned methane and

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:49.130
then hexane, which was six carbons.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:55.000
Here I have essentially one carbon,
two carbons, three, four, five.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.930
And we can see the temperatures
are increasing as well.

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:04.660
So if we were to put all those on a chart,
the bigger it gets, the higher it goes.

00:09:05.209 --> 00:09:07.250
So what can you try to say about that?

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:12.460
Well, from heaviest to lightest, the
heaviest one has the strongest force

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:17.529
of attraction to other molecules
of the same type, making them a

00:09:17.529 --> 00:09:20.730
lot more difficult to separate.

00:09:21.349 --> 00:09:25.700
So larger molecules have higher
polarizability, which makes them

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:32.210
have essentially a stronger force of
attraction and more likely to be a liquid

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:34.600
or even a solid the bigger it gets.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:39.529
One way to think of it too when it comes
to organic molecules like these, methane,

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.800
propane and butane are all gases, right?

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:46.750
Propane you find in stoves or
butane you might find in a lighter.

00:09:46.819 --> 00:09:50.439
It's generally a gas and starts to
become a liquid as you go through

00:09:50.439 --> 00:09:51.770
depending on how much pressure it has.

00:09:52.474 --> 00:09:55.165
Whereas, let's say octane,
which is what you fill your

00:09:55.344 --> 00:09:57.094
car with, is a liquid, right?

00:09:57.124 --> 00:09:58.824
You know, we call it gasoline in the US.

00:09:59.094 --> 00:09:59.724
It's a liquid.

00:10:00.314 --> 00:10:03.365
Um, and the farther and farther you go
is when you eventually start becoming

00:10:03.385 --> 00:10:08.454
a solid, when you add enough of these
carbons to the molecule, you get more and

00:10:08.454 --> 00:10:11.454
more attracted to each other as you go.

00:10:12.405 --> 00:10:16.244
So just to kind of practice this concept,
well, predict which one would have

00:10:16.244 --> 00:10:18.555
the strongest intermolecular force.

00:10:19.165 --> 00:10:23.625
So I have two molecules here, I
have hydrogen and oxygen, both of

00:10:23.625 --> 00:10:27.114
them are linear, meaning they're
going to be non polar diatomics.

00:10:27.215 --> 00:10:30.944
Every diatomic is non polar,
which is one of the most

00:10:30.944 --> 00:10:32.415
important things to keep in mind.

00:10:33.084 --> 00:10:34.405
Which one is more?

00:10:34.595 --> 00:10:38.084
Well, hydrogen, right, is One
of your smallest elements.

00:10:38.435 --> 00:10:41.334
So when we're trying to look at
it, right, the first thing you

00:10:41.334 --> 00:10:42.625
want to do is, are they not polar?

00:10:42.975 --> 00:10:44.055
As I just said, they are.

00:10:44.855 --> 00:10:47.655
The next thing we're going to look at
is, well, let's see if we can figure

00:10:47.655 --> 00:10:49.775
out the sizes of each one of them.

00:10:50.425 --> 00:10:54.454
Well, if we want to look at the
sizes, well, hydrogen looks like

00:10:54.454 --> 00:10:55.745
the Lewis structure on the left.

00:10:56.285 --> 00:10:57.675
Oxygen looks like the one on the right.

00:10:58.255 --> 00:11:00.905
They look about identical when
it comes to electronegativity.

00:11:01.515 --> 00:11:05.345
So when we look at both of
them, right, oxygen has a molar

00:11:05.345 --> 00:11:06.975
mass of 32 grams per mole.

00:11:07.719 --> 00:11:14.129
Hydrogen has a molar mass of two, so it's
two electrons versus about 16 electrons.

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:20.879
That's a huge difference between the
two, meaning that hydrogen, the odds of

00:11:20.879 --> 00:11:26.040
the electrons being even is pretty high,
where something like oxygen are such a

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:32.010
big volume here, there's such a large area
for the polarizability to occur that's

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:34.350
gonna lead to a stronger dispersion force.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:38.210
So oxygen is going to be
a lot higher as a result.

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:42.640
Okay, so you're looking at how bigger,
which one's bigger, is the sum of this.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:44.699
Let's look at this next one.

00:11:45.259 --> 00:11:48.389
So CF4 and CBr4.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:52.750
Well, fluorine is, so it's pretty much
carbon, carbon, they're the same size.

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:54.939
So fluorine versus bromine.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:58.170
Fluorine is in the top right
corner of your periodic table.

00:11:58.220 --> 00:11:59.950
Bromine is a little bit lower.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:03.450
So which one would have the
stronger dispersion force?

00:12:04.189 --> 00:12:08.959
These are both non polar, right, because
they both are tetrahedral geometries.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:11.860
The heavier of the two is
going to be bromine, right?

00:12:12.409 --> 00:12:17.960
So CBr4 is going to have a
much larger volume to it.

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:21.719
It's going to be more electrons,
more dispersion that's going to be

00:12:21.719 --> 00:12:23.620
occurring, a higher polarizability.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:28.070
So out of these two, the one that's
going to be stronger is going to be CBr4.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:30.710
It's going to be the key here.

00:12:31.499 --> 00:12:34.200
Now, to sum up everything we talked about.

00:12:34.615 --> 00:12:36.375
How do we keep dispersion correct?

00:12:37.185 --> 00:12:39.215
All molecules do this force.

00:12:39.265 --> 00:12:43.805
It's considered to be a universal
attractive force between all of them.

00:12:44.605 --> 00:12:48.434
So this is something that they all
can do as their, this temporary

00:12:48.435 --> 00:12:52.354
dipoles just because electrons are
not even between the molecules.

00:12:52.959 --> 00:12:57.990
However, it's essentially the
most prominent force for, for

00:12:57.990 --> 00:12:59.700
anything that's non polar.

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:03.550
So whenever we're looking at these, how
do we figure out which one's bigger?

00:13:03.579 --> 00:13:07.319
Well, it's generally based on the
number of electrons, which means more

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:09.199
valence shells, the larger it is.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:13.380
So we want to look at which one's
the biggest of all, what's the

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:15.790
heaviest in terms of molar mass.

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:18.500
So look at your molar mass,
figure out what's the heaviest.

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:20.780
And that is going to be your answer.

00:13:21.819 --> 00:13:25.170
So now keep in mind though, when you
have, let's say, stronger intermolecular

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:28.790
forces, they will override dispersion.

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:31.400
So if you have something that's
polar, that automatically is going

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:33.100
to be strong, stronger interaction.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:36.200
Um, so we always look at
intermolecular force first,

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:39.179
then we look at size afterwards.

00:13:39.989 --> 00:13:43.180
Um, and that's what we're looking
at for intermolecular forces.

00:13:43.755 --> 00:13:47.635
I hope this video helped you trying to
understand part two of our video here.

00:13:48.195 --> 00:13:51.925
So thank you so much for watching
and please stick around for

00:13:52.105 --> 00:13:53.705
more on intermolecular forces.

